            <div xml:id="d963">

                <pb facs="0854" n="738" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg738"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/3057.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the American Consul at Chihuahua</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-03-06T16:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 6, 1912—4 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your March 5. The Embassy, in advising Americans to leave Chihuahua, is acting
                    upon information from your Consulate as to conditions in your district, and
                    must, from the very nature of the case, therefore, depend upon your discretion
                    both as to the necessity of warning Americans to leave particular localities and
                    the means of conveying this warning to them.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Huntington Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
